sec.p:
In the population with available galectin‐3 concentrations at baseline, 314 composite kidney events were observed. There was a significant increasing gradient of risk for the kidney‐EP across quartiles of galectin‐3 (Figure

1A
). This pattern was directionally consistent for individual components of the composite endpoint: ≥40% eGFR decrease (3.27% vs. 1.06%), end‐stage renal disease (0.29% vs. 0.03%) and renal death (0.3% vs. 0.0%). Adjusting for clinical characteristics, higher galectin‐3 concentration was independently associated with higher risk for the kidney‐EP (Figure

1A
). The c‐statistic without galectin‐3 was 0.72 and with galectin‐3 was 0.73.

In this nested biomarker cohort, dapagliflozin significantly reduced the risk of the kidney‐EP [hazard ratio (HR) 0.45 (95% CI 0.23, 0.85), P < 0.001], consistent with its effect in the overall trial population. This relative benefit was consistent across all galectin‐3 quartiles (P < 0.001 for all quartiles, P interaction = 0.97; Figure

1B
). Additionally, the absolute risk reduction achieved with dapagliflozin increased across galectin‐3 quartiles (P trend = 0.048). From baseline to 6 months, dapagliflozin did not affect galectin‐3 concentration in subsets of patients randomized to dapagliflozin (n = 264) or placebo [n = 248; ratio of geometric means (95% CI) 1.01 (0.97, 1.06) P = 0.60].
